## Instruments

    tools/radsrv.py   the RADIUS server, run as an ordinary user on 11812/11813 (no root, the
                      box's system freeradius untouched). One log line per packet.
    tools/radsend.py  used in T33407; not needed here.
    tools/radwire.py  decodes and counts the RADIUS packets in a tcpdump pcap and verifies
                      each reply's Response Authenticator.
    tools/conlogin.py NEW for this case, and now a repo tool with a README entry: it logs a
                      console OUT and back IN as a named user and reports
                      `LOGIN RESULT: PRIV|USER|FAIL|TIMEOUT`. `aaa authentication login` only
                      runs when somebody really logs in, so a real login attempt is the only
                      instrument that can exercise it -- no `show` command can. It always logs
                      back in as a fallback user afterwards. Registered in `tools/tb`'s console
                      list so it inherits the console-held-by-another-process refusal.

Server command used for the measurement (work/radsrv4-tail.log is its log):

    tools/tb tool tb277 ckaaa radsrv --bind 10.38.22.33 --auth-port 11812 --acct-port 11813 \
      --secret testing123 --client 10.38.22.34 --client 10.38.22.66 \
      --user radclient1:radpass123 --user manager:friend \
      --reply-attr 6=0x00000006 \
      --reply-attr 26=0x0000000901137368656c6c3a707269762d6c766c3d3135 \
      --log /tmp/ckaaa/radsrv4.log --seconds 2400 --stopfile /tmp/ckaaa/stop4

(`6=0x00000006` is Service-Type = Administrative; the 26 attribute is the Cisco-style VSA
`shell:priv-lvl=15`. Why they are there is STEP 4 below.)

## STEP 1 (designed) -- the instrument check: a local login still works

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user manager --password friend
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'

**Measured platform fact:** an AW+ console login lands at privilege 1 (`awplus>`) even for
`username manager privilege 15`; `enable` then raises it to 15 (`show privilege` ->
"Current privilege level is 15"). So the success criterion for a console login test on this
platform is reaching `>` (conlogin `USER`), not `#`. A first draft of this step expected `#`
and would have graded a perfectly good login as a mismatch. STEP 1 verdict: PASS.

