    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user radclient1 --password definitely-wrong --expect fail
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=radclient1  seconds=1.4  prompt='awplus login:'   (MATCH)

    server: 09:27:17 Access-Request  from 10.38.22.34 id=254 user='radclient1' ...
            09:27:17 Access-Reject   to 10.38.22.34 id=254 user='radclient1' (wrong password)
    wire:   52.284 Access-Request id=254 ... / 52.285 Access-Reject id=254 Reply-Message='Rejected: wrong password' [authenticator VALID]

The request reached the server and the server rejected it -- so this is a RADIUS rejection,
not a local lockout. STEP 6 verdict: PASS.

### 🔴 FINDING -- `local` in the method list is a fallback for an UNREACHABLE server, NOT for a Reject

This cost the console a login and is the most useful thing this case learned.

With `aaa authentication login default group radius local` in force and the server **answering**
with an Access-Reject for `manager` (because the test server did not know that user), the DUT
honoured the Reject and did **not** try the local database:

    09:22:57 Access-Request from 10.38.22.34 id=134 user='manager' ...
    09:22:57 Access-Reject  to 10.38.22.34 id=134 user='manager' (no such user)
    console: `Login incorrect`

That is correct RFC behaviour -- a Reject is authoritative -- but the practical consequence on
a bench is severe: **the moment the method list points at RADIUS, every account you still need
must exist on the RADIUS server, or you lose the console.** Writing `local` at the end of the
list does not protect you. The two safe patterns are (a) the RADIUS server knows the
administrative account too, or (b) the RADIUS server is absent, in which case the fallback does
engage (STEP 7). The console was recovered by stopping the test server, with no reload, no
power cycle and 2 failed logins on `manager` -- well below the 5 that lock an account.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- control: an UNREACHABLE server, and the measured fallback delay

Server stopped; everything else unchanged.

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user manager --password friend --settle 90
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=25.5  prompt='awplus>'

    DUT log: 2026 Oct 19 20:24:14 authpriv.err awplus login: pam_radius_auth: RADIUS server 10.38.22.33 failed to respond
             2026 Oct 19 20:24:29 authpriv.err awplus login: pam_radius_auth: All RADIUS servers failed to respond.

**25.5 s against 0.4 s for the same credentials with a plain `local` method list** -- the DUT
tried RADIUS first, waited out `Timeout 5 sec` x (1 + `Retransmit Count 3`) = 20 s, and then
fell through to `local`. The fallback works, and its cost is measurable.
A practical note for any future AAA case on this box: a console-login tool's settle timeout
must exceed that 25.5 s, or a successful fallback login is misread as a TIMEOUT. It happened
once here (conlogin's 25 s default) and is why `--settle` exists.
STEP 7 verdict: PASS.

## STEP 8 (designed) -- control: a MISMATCHED shared key

Server restarted with the correct secret; the DUT's key changed to a different one:

    awplus(config)# radius-server host 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813 key wrongkey99

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user radclient1 --password radpass123 --settle 90 --expect fail
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=radclient1  seconds=21.9  prompt='awplus login:'   (MATCH)

Server side -- the requests ARRIVE and are silently discarded, which is what RFC 3579 requires
of a packet whose Message-Authenticator does not verify:

    09:27:55 DROP-bad-msg-auth from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:00 DROP-bad-msg-auth from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:05 DROP-bad-msg-auth from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:10 DROP-bad-msg-auth from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'

Wire -- the same request id and the same Message-Authenticator, four times, 5.003 s apart:

      90.220  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request id=183 len=95 ...
      95.223  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request id=183 len=95 ...
     100.228  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request id=183 len=95 ...

**That is `Timeout 5 sec` and `Retransmit Count 3` measured on the wire**, and it is the first
time in this group that the configured timer values have been confirmed rather than read off a
`show` command. The login then fell through to `local`, where `radclient1` has no account, so
it was refused at 21.9 s. Accounting packets with the wrong key were rejected the same way, via
the Accounting Request Authenticator (`DROP-bad-acct-auth ... (wrong secret)`, 4 of them).

Note what the DUT cannot distinguish: its own log says `pam_radius_auth: RADIUS server
10.38.22.33 failed to respond` for BOTH this case and STEP 7's genuinely absent server. A
silently-discarded packet and a dead server look identical from the NAS, by design.
STEP 8 verdict: PASS. The DUT's key was restored to `testing123` immediately afterwards.

## STEP 9 (designed) -- removal

    awplus(config)# aaa authentication login default local
    awplus(config)# no aaa accounting login default
    awplus(config)# no radius-server host 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813

    show radius                         -> "No RADIUS servers currently defined"
    show running-config | include aaa|radius ->
        aaa authentication enable default local
        aaa authentication login default local        (and no radius-server line at all)

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user radclient1 --password radpass123 --expect fail
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=radclient1  seconds=2.1  prompt='awplus login:'   (MATCH)

    radwire /tmp/ckaaa/t33408-removal.pcap -> 0 RADIUS packet(s)
    radsrv log: nothing after 09:29:53, i.e. nothing at all after the removal

Two independent instruments both say zero, and both had given non-zero minutes earlier on the
same path. Also note the 2.1 s refusal against 21.9 s in STEP 8: with no server configured the
DUT does not wait for a timeout. STEP 9 verdict: PASS.

