`show log tail 200`, filtered to this case's window (2026 Oct 19 20:17-20:33 UTC) and to lines
that are NOT this session's own IMISH command echo. Every line below is this case's own
traffic and matches a packet or a deliberate step:

    20:20:35 authpriv.notice login: pam_unix(console:auth): check pass; user unknown
    20:20:37 authpriv.notice login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM ttyS0 FOR (unknown), User not known to the underlying authentication module
    20:20:48 authpriv.notice login: FAILED LOGIN 2 FROM ttyS0 FOR (unknown), ...
    20:22:36 authpriv.err    login: Invalid user name "radclient1". Abort.
    20:22:41 daemon.notice   systemd[1]: serial-getty@ttyS0.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=1/FAILURE
    20:22:41 daemon.warning  systemd[1]: serial-getty@ttyS0.service: Failed with result 'exit-code'.
    20:22:44 authpriv.notice login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM ttyS0 FOR manager, Authentication failure
    20:24:14 authpriv.err    login: pam_radius_auth: RADIUS server 10.38.22.33 failed to respond
    20:24:29 authpriv.err    login: pam_radius_auth: All RADIUS servers failed to respond.
    20:27:05 authpriv.notice login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM ttyS0 FOR radclient1, Authentication failure
    20:27:40 / 20:27:46 / 20:28:01  pam_radius_auth: … failed to respond  (the STEP 8 key mismatch)
    20:28:03 authpriv.notice login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM ttyS0 FOR radclient1, Authentication failure

Read as follows:

  * the `FAILED LOGIN n` lines are the case's own deliberate negative legs. **The highest
    counter reached was 2**, against the 5 that lock an AW+ account, so no account was locked
    at any point -- which also disposes of the lockout hypothesis raised mid-run: every
    control's Access-Request is present in the server log, so the DUT always asked RADIUS and
    was never short-circuited by a local lockout.
  * `serial-getty@ttyS0.service: Main process exited, status=1/FAILURE` is the console getty
    respawning after `login` aborted the `radclient1` session in STEP 4. It is a direct and
    explained consequence of this case's own step, not a spontaneous fault; the console came
    straight back with a `login:` banner, which is how the case continued.
  * **No `kernel:` line, no crash, no `.crit`/`.alert`/`.emerg`, no exception, and no daemon
    restart this case did not cause.** The two pre-existing `kern.err boardinfo-eeprom-i2c`
    lines are dated 2026 Oct 16 and are outside this window.

**Health verdict: clean.** The run is labelled **clean**, not confounded.

